    "lawCode": "Amd §97-g, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6046               REVISED 06/23/09\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to authorizing the\naggregate purchases of energy for state agencies, institutions, local\ngovernments, public authorities and public benefit corporations; and\nproviding for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to expand the authority of the Office of\nGeneral Services (OGS) to purchase and deliver electricity and\nrenewable energy as centralized services to State agencies.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend State Finance Law § 97-g(4) to\nauthorize OGS to purchase and deliver renewable energy and renewable\nenergy credits or attributes on behalf of State agencies; to authorize\nOGS to make such purchases, along with the purchase of electricity,\nfrom suppliers in addition to the Power Authority of the State of New\nYork. Section 1 would also clarify OGS authority to purchase fuels\nthat include alternative fuels as well as gasoline and diesel fuel on\nbehalf of State agencies.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would require OGS to prepare a report to the\nGovernor, Speaker of the Assembly and Temporary President of the\nSenate on its aggregate electric procurement program and submit it on\nFebruary 15, 2011.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date,\nand expiration on July 31, 2011.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nState Finance Law § 97-g authorizes OGS to procure and provide certain\nservices and commodities to state agencies. Existing law does not\nexpressly authorize the provision of renewable energy or renewable\nenergy credits or attributes.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nA similar bill, S.5730, was introduced in 2006 but did not advance out\nof committee.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThis bill would authorize OGS to purchase and deliver, as centralized\nservices, renewable energy and renewable energy credits and\nattributes, along with electricity, from the New York Power Authority\nand other suppliers. Rather than relying on the delivered price for\nsuch services from a servicing utility, the bill would allow OGS to\nleverage the value of aggregated purchasing by the State. OGS would\neffectuate these purchases through specific annual internal service\nfund appropriations. The benefitted State agencies that receive such\ncentralized services would continue to reimburse the internal service\nfund.\n\nSimilarly, the bill would clarify that OGS may supply to State\nagencies alternative fuels, such as biodiesel and compressed natural\ngas, for motor vehicle fleets. This allows the benefitted agency to\navoid expending resources on duplicative infrastructure to service\nalternative fueled vehicles.\n\nThis bill is expected to result in significant savings for the State\nand for public benefit corporations and public authorities. For\nexample, during one recent 12-month period ending in November 2008,\nOGS alone avoided over $1.24 million in electric commodity costs\n(approximately 4 percent of the value) by purchasing electricity\ndirectly through the New York Independent Systems Operator rather than\nfrom the local electric utility.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nCost containment and potential savings would accrue to State agencies\nthat receive centralized services with annual avoided costs\napproaching $4 million.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately and would sunset on July 31,\n2011.\n",
